From 26ad7c7d840ddd609817fab56d0f1428f5f76afa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: barisyild <25794892+barisyild@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 05:21:13 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Fix inverted return in hasSideEffects, which was deleting
 if-statement bodies
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`hasSideEffects` returns true to mean "has side effects" in every branch but
one. The composite branch — covering TBlock, TIf, TVar, TSwitch and the rest —
computed a local named `isPure` and returned it unnegated, inverting the
function's meaning for exactly those expressions.

The consequence is severe and silent. `blockElement` uses the result to decide
whether an `if` with no `else` can be replaced by its condition alone:

    case TIf(e1, e2, null) if(!hasSideEffects(e2)):
        return blockElement(acc, [e1].concat(tail));

With the inverted result, a body full of assignments and calls was reported as
side-effect free, so the body was dropped and only the condition survived. No
error, no warning: the compiled program simply takes the other path.

It also explains the shape of the symptom. A single-statement body is not a
TBlock and never reaches the inverted branch, so `if (c) one();` survived while
`if (c) { two(); statements(); }` did not — which reads like a size limit and
is really a type dispatch.

Found while recompiling a PlayStation game to Haxe: guard clauses of the form
`if (cond) { ...; return; }` compiled to their fall-through path, so functions
did the opposite of what they read as.
---
 .../implementations/RemovePureExpressionsImpl.hx    | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/reflaxe/preprocessors/implementations/RemovePureExpressionsImpl.hx b/src/reflaxe/preprocessors/implementations/RemovePureExpressionsImpl.hx
index ee39c97..5a49716 100644
--- a/src/reflaxe/preprocessors/implementations/RemovePureExpressionsImpl.hx
+++ b/src/reflaxe/preprocessors/implementations/RemovePureExpressionsImpl.hx
@@ -209,13 +209,24 @@ private class OptimizerTexpr {
 				TSwitch(_), TArrayDecl(_), TBlock(_),
 				TObjectDecl(_), TVar(_):
 			{
+				// recompsx: this returned `isPure`, inverting the function's own meaning.
+				//
+				// Every other branch of `hasSideEffects` returns true to mean "has side
+				// effects". This one computed purity and returned it unnegated, so a block
+				// containing assignments and calls was reported as side-effect *free* — and
+				// `blockElement` below then replaced `if (cond) { ...body... }` with just
+				// `cond`, silently deleting the body. That is the single most damaging defect
+				// we have hit: no error, no warning, and the program takes the other path.
+				//
+				// It also explains why a one-statement body survived: that is not a TBlock and
+				// never reaches this branch.
 				var isPure = true;
 				TypedExprTools.iter(expr, function(subExpr) {
 					if(hasSideEffects(subExpr)) {
 						isPure = false;
 					}
 				});
-				return isPure;
+				return !isPure;
 			}
 			case _: {
 				return false;
-- 
